INT. REPOSSESSED PIANO SHOWROOM - NIGHT

Rows of grand pianos under sheets, like ghosts at a recital. One lamp burns on a bench. The air smells of lacquer and dust.

DETECTIVE HORTENSE LAFLEUR (50s, trench coat, a limp she hides) circles a closed Steinway. At the far wall, a heavyset man, CLEM BOUDREAUX (40s, pinstripe shirt, nervous), twists a ring on his finger. Beside him, a girl in an oversized sweatshirt, WREN (15), picks at the lid of a baby grand.

HORTENSE
Mr. Boudreaux.

CLEM
Detective.

HORTENSE
You recognize that piano?

CLEM
It's a Steinway. I recognize most Steinways.

HORTENSE
It came off a truck in Baton Rouge on the ninth.

CLEM
Plenty came off trucks on the ninth.

HORTENSE
Not with a body inside.

Wren looks up sharply. Clem stops twisting the ring.

CLEM
Wren, honey, go wait in the car.

WREN
I'm not leaving you, Uncle Clem.

HORTENSE
Your niece?

CLEM
My niece.

HORTENSE
Hello, Wren. I'm Hortense.

Wren says nothing. Hortense lifts the sheet and raps on the closed lid. A hollow thud.

HORTENSE (CONT'D)
Open it, please.

CLEM
It's locked.

HORTENSE
Then unlock it.

CLEM
I don't have the key.

HORTENSE
Wren does.

Wren's eyes flick to her uncle. Clem exhales and shuts his eyes.

DETECTIVE LAFLEUR
(softly, to the girl)
It's all right, sweetheart. Nobody's in trouble tonight who doesn't deserve to be.

Wren slides a small brass key from her sleeve and walks to the Steinway. The lock turns with a click that echoes through the whole room.
